----------------------------------------Linux User Authentication and Sudo Permissions------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. User Authentication
Authentication means verifying that a user is who they claim to be.

In Linux, authentication commonly involves:
Username
   ↓
Password
   ↓
Authentication system
   ↓
Access granted / denied

Example:
su - madhav
Linux asks for Madhav's password and authenticates the user.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Important Authentication Files

Linux stores user account information mainly in:
/etc/passwd
/etc/shadow
/etc/group
/etc/passwd

Contains basic information about user accounts.

View it:
cat /etc/passwd

Example:
madhav:x:1001:1001:Madhav:/home/madhav:/bin/bash

The fields represent:
Username
Password placeholder
UID
GID
User information
Home directory
Login shell

The x means the password information is stored in /etc/shadow.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. /etc/shadow

Contains password hashes and password-related information.

View it:
sudo cat /etc/shadow

A normal user generally cannot read this file.

Check its permissions:
ls -l /etc/shadow

Typical permissions are restrictive:
-rw-r-----

Only privileged users should be able to access password hashes.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Checking User Information

Current user:
whoami

User ID and groups:
id

For a specific user:
id madhav

Check whether a user exists:
getent passwd madhav

Check groups:
groups madhav

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Password Management

Set/change a user's password:
sudo passwd madhav

Change your own password:
passwd

Lock an account:
sudo passwd -l madhav

Unlock an account:
sudo passwd -u madhav

Check password/account status:
sudo passwd -S madhav

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. User Login Shell

Check the user's shell:
getent passwd madhav

Example:
madhav:x:1001:1001::/home/madhav:/bin/bash

The last field is the login shell:
/bin/bash

Change the shell:
sudo usermod -s /bin/bash madhav

To prevent interactive login, a common shell is:
/usr/sbin/nologin

Example:
sudo usermod -s /usr/sbin/nologin madhav

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. What is sudo?

sudo means Superuser Do.
It allows an authorized user to execute commands with elevated privileges.

Example:
sudo apt update

Without sudo, a normal user may not have permission to perform administrative operations.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

8. Check Whether You Have Sudo Access

Run:
sudo -l

This displays the commands the current user is allowed to run with sudo.

You can also check your groups:

groups
On many Debian-based systems, administrative access is commonly granted through the sudo group.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

9. Add a User to the Sudo Group

For example:
sudo usermod -aG sudo madhav

Check:
groups madhav

You may see:
madhav sudo

Madhav may need to log out and log back in before the new group membership is reflected in a new session.

Then test:
su - madhav
sudo whoami

Expected:
root

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

10. Sudo Configuration

The main configuration file is:
/etc/sudoers

Do not normally edit it directly with nano or another editor.

Use:
sudo visudo

visudo checks the syntax before saving, helping prevent a broken sudo configuration.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

11. Sudoers Format

A basic rule looks like:
user host=(run-as-user) command

Example:
madhav ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL

Meaning Madhav can run commands through sudo as authorized by this rule.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

12. Giving Sudo Access to a Group

Instead of giving permissions individually, Linux commonly uses groups.

Example:
%sudo ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL

The % means the entry refers to a group.

Therefore:

%sudo

means the sudo group.

A user added to that group can receive the permissions defined by the group rule.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

13. Allowing a Specific Command

You can give a user permission to run only a specific command.

Example:
madhav ALL=(root) /usr/bin/systemctl restart apache2

This is more restrictive than:
madhav ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL

The principle is:
Give users only the privileges they actually need.
This is called the principle of least privilege.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

14. Running a Command as Another User

Run as root:
sudo command

Run a shell as root:
sudo -i

Check:
whoami

Output:
root

Run a command as another user:
sudo -u madhav whoami

Output:
madhav

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

15. su vs sudo
su

Switch user:
su - madhav

Switch to root:
su -

sudo
Run a specific command with elevated privileges:

sudo command
Main difference:

su
 ↓
Switch to another user

sudo
 ↓
Run a command with elevated privileges

For administration, sudo is generally preferred because it can provide more controlled and auditable privilege escalation.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

16. Sudo Authentication

When you run:
sudo apt update

Linux may ask for your own user's password, depending on the sudo configuration.

Example:
[sudo] password for madhav:

It is not necessarily asking for the root password.
After successful authentication, sudo executes the command according to the user's sudo permissions.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

17. Check Sudo Logs

Sudo activity can be useful for security monitoring.
On many Debian-based systems, authentication-related events can be viewed with:

sudo journalctl _COMM=sudo

You can also search authentication logs:
sudo grep sudo /var/log/auth.log

This is particularly useful for SOC Analyst work because sudo activity can show privilege escalation attempts.
